The Week 6 lab’s solution is to code a static array solution and vector
solution to perform calculations on a collection of student grades.
For this week’s Participation Activity, submit a simple cost/benefit
analysis of using vectors instead of arrays. This can be as simple as
listing pros vs cons.


Arrays and Vectors are vastly different, yet also functionally similar, and each have their own weaknesses and strenghts. With an array, for example, the static nature of them allows a lot of easy calculational shortcuts, such as just using the integer 10 for a fixed list of 10 items, rather than constantly needing to retreive the number of values. Yet using vectors instead of arrays allows for much greater flexibility, for a wide variety of sitations where a dataset can change in size. This increased functionality comes at a price though, as they can be more compliated than a simple array. Thankfully, there are different syntaxes that can be used to easily append or remove values from a vector, and the complexity isnt a super steep hill to overcome. They are considered higher level, so they need a higher level of understanding to work with, but when done right, can be an increcibly poweful tool for a variety of programming situations. They are slightly slower, but just slightly, and the benefits far outweigh the costs in my opinion.